import { can, type Perm } from "@/lib/access";
import type { Profile } from "@/lib/types";

// How to use AMBIT Intelligence, written for the assistant. Each section says who it's for, and the
// assistant only ever gets the sections the person asking can use: contractors never see staff screens.
// Keep this in step with the app when screens change (README.md has the same features in more detail).

// ops: "crm" = contractors in an AMBIT-OPS workspace who work leads (not crews); "field" = crews and subs.
type Section = { who: "all" | "staff" | "customer" | "admin"; perm?: Perm; anyPerm?: Perm[]; ops?: "crm" | "field"; title: string; body: string };

// Takeoff works the same for AMBIT staff and for contractors, so both sections share these steps.
const TAKEOFF = `1. "New takeoff": job name and customer, then Create.
2. Drag the plan PDFs onto the screen (or "Upload plans"). Each page becomes a sheet; sheet numbers (A2.1) and the scale written on the sheet (1/4" = 1'-0", 1" = 20') are read automatically when the PDF has text.
3. Check the scale. If a sheet has no scale, a yellow bar says so: "Set scale", then either pick the scale from the list or draw a line over a dimension you know and type its length (e.g. 24'-6"). Always check one known dimension before measuring.
4. Read plan set with AI (the purple button at the top right): tick the sheets (all by default) and type the builder if you know it, then "Read". The AI reads four sheets at a time (a minute or two each) and draws every insulated surface itself (the 18 assemblies in AMBIT's measuring guide: slab, exterior walls, garage partition walls, knee walls, attic demising walls, attic ceilings, under decking, vaulted, cathedral and sloped ceilings, roof line, garage roof line, garage ceiling, floor over ambient, floor over garage, foundation walls, rim joists), plus windows and doors (purple and blue dots labeled by side: F front, B back, L left, R right). Its shapes are dashed until someone checks them. Walls, ceilings and floors are counted from floor plans, slab from the foundation plan (or the first floor), roof lines from the roof plan; shapes on elevations and sections are dotted, for reference only, never counted. Then it checks the sheets against each other: the Alerts tab lists what disagrees (a window on one sheet only, a height that differs, a scale that doesn't check out), each with its sheets (tap one to open it) and "Checked it"; the Windows tab lists every window and door once with its size and area, and "Take out of exterior walls" subtracts their area from the walls. On each read sheet the AI assistant card (top of the Items tab) says what it found and what it assumed: "Accept N on this sheet" makes its shapes solid (editing a shape's corners also accepts it), "Discard" removes its unchecked shapes (Undo brings them back), the arrow reads the sheet again (only shapes nobody checked are replaced). "Tell the AI what it got wrong on A1.0": type it the way you'd tell a new estimator, pick "Just this time", "Remember for all plans" or "Remember for this builder", then "Fix this sheet": it reads the sheet again with that correction, and remembering saves it as a rule used on every future reading. [Takeoff > AI rules](/takeoff/rules) lists every rule (where it was learned, who, when): edit, switch off (kept, not used) or delete; add one by hand; Export (JSON or Markdown) everything taught; Import rules from PRO-Rate's Export (JSON). AMBIT's rules apply to every takeoff; a contractor's own rules only to theirs. It also keeps every correction and a log of every AI call. Solo: the target button on an item shows only that item's shapes. Templates: on a takeoff's Report page, "Save as template" (for a plan built over and over); on the Takeoff page "Start from" a template copies its plans, shapes and quantities (no AI): check and adjust.
4b. Quick scan (optional, no shapes): "Quick scan" (or "Let AI read the plans first"), tick the sheets to read (floor plans, sections and energy notes help most; about 20 seconds a sheet), then "Read". It follows AMBIT's takeoff measuring guide. It lists the insulation called out (with the plans' exact words), roof pitches, wall heights, framing, the ceilings (vaulted, cathedral, tray, sloped, room ceiling heights), the foundation (slab, crawlspace, basement) and rim joist, floors over the garage or outdoors, an HVAC platform, the garage, porch and attic, and a scale check for each sheet (a sheet marked NOT TO SCALE, a scale that doesn't match what's written, or a long dimension to check it with). Then a few questions: is the garage part of the conditioned house, is the attic sealed (foam at the roof deck) or vented, are the porch and garage attics walled off, the pitch (10/10, the same as 12/12, when the plans don't list one), foundation, stories, framing and wall heights. It then lists the items to set up, with the team's starting heights (9' walls, 4' knee walls, 8' foundation walls, 11 1/4" rim joist) where the plans don't say: exterior walls (one per wall height), garage partition walls, knee walls (their height is the step between two ceiling heights when the plans show one), roof line or attic ceiling, attic demising walls, garage roof line and garage ceiling (a sealed attic over the garage), under decking, vaulted, cathedral, tray and sloped ceilings (area × pitch), rim joist to outdoors and to the attic, foundation walls, slab (area and perimeter, the first floor's conditioned footprint only), floor over garage and floor over outdoors. Each says why. Untick any you don't want, rename them, "Set up N items". "How AMBIT measures" at the bottom has the rules to trace by. You still trace every shape, and check depths and R-values: AI can misread small print.
5. Or "New item" and pick a preset (every assembly in AMBIT's measuring guide: Exterior walls, Garage partition walls, Knee walls, Attic demising wall, Foundation walls, Rim joist to outdoors or to attic, Attic ceiling (flat), Under decking, Vaulted, Cathedral or Sloped ceiling, Roof line, Garage roof line, Garage ceiling, Slab, Floor over garage, Floor over outdoors, Can lights) or make your own with material, thickness and R-value. Items can have a work area (for the quote). Areas can have a roof pitch (rise per 12); walls have a wall height and openings to subtract, or the "Gable (attic demising wall)" shape: trace the bottom of the wall and its triangle is worked out from the pitch.
6. Measure: Area: click each corner, click the first point to close. Walls / lengths: click along the wall, double-click or Enter to finish. Count: click each one. Cutout (X) takes an opening out of an area.
7. Fix a shape: with Select (V), click a shape to select it; drag a corner dot to move it; click anywhere on its edge to add a new corner there (and drag it); double-click a corner to remove it.
8. Undo and Redo (buttons at the top, or Ctrl/⌘ Z and Ctrl/⌘ Shift Z): go back or forward up to 60 steps (adding or deleting a shape, moving corners, adding, changing or deleting an item, setting the scale). Hover a button to see what it will undo or redo.
9. Totals (square feet, feet, board feet for spray foam, counts) update on the right. "Report" gives totals by item, material and sheet; "Print / Save PDF" and "Excel". "Material order" turns it into an AMBIT order.
Keys: V select, H move, S scale, A area, L linear, C count, X cutout, Enter finish, Esc cancel, Backspace removes the last point, Shift for straight lines, Space or scroll to move and zoom, Ctrl/⌘ Z undo, Ctrl/⌘ Shift Z or Ctrl Y redo.
Reading plans: sheet numbers start with a letter for the discipline (A architectural, S structural, M mechanical, E electrical, P plumbing); floor plans and roof plans give areas, building sections and wall sections show assemblies and ceiling heights, and energy or general notes list required R-values. The scale applies to the drawing it's written under; details on the same sheet can have their own scale.`;

